JQPvgbv-StgkTsGHPuQb65-rlI9mZkRnjz1NKJjbHUTfIcexxwo0AY89a1_mGCyj8CxhbUZJ-jHCiA2cttrAm3PozxdSFZsvqBEKCe13d8WP1XO2cC31OZFr5w","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"META\n- SEO Title: StablR Stablecoin Exploit: Attacker Mints $13.5M\n- Meta Description: The StablR stablecoin exploit has frozen USDR and EURR trading after an attacker minted $13.5M in unbacked tokens, violating MiCA's 1:1 backing rule.\n- URL Slug: stablr-stablecoin-exploit-freezes-tokens\n- Categories: Stablecoins, Security, Regulation\n- Primary Keyword: StablR stablecoin exploit\n- Secondary Keywords: MiCA regulation, stablecoin depeg, multisig wallet security\n---\n# StablR Stablecoin Exploit Freezes USDR, EURR After $13.5M Breach\n\nThe **StablR stablecoin exploit** has forced the European issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its USDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According to a [statement from the company](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/26/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens), the move came after an attacker leveraged a critical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked tokens.\n\nThe breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident places StablR’s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the European Union’s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into full effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens are no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA’s core requirements. Exchanges have been requested to halt trading while StablR, with the help of [external cybersecurity firms](https://beincrypto.com/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit/), investigates the breach.\n\n## How a Weak Multisig Led to a $13.5M Exploit\n\nThe root cause of the attack was a fundamental weakness in StablR’s smart contract architecture on Ethereum. Blockchain security firm GoPlus Security identified a poorly configured multisignature wallet as the single point of failure. This wallet, responsible for minting new tokens, used a 1-of-3 signature threshold. This meant any transaction could be approved by just one of the three authorized signers, offering minimal protection.\n\nThe attacker compromised one of these keys, granting them unilateral control. They added their own address as an administrator, removed the legitimate signers, and seized the minting mechanism. With this control, the attacker minted 8.35 million USDR and 4.5 million EURR. These illicitly created tokens, worth $13.5 million on paper, immediately diluted the value of the legitimately backed supply. The attacker then attempted to sell the tokens on decentralized exchanges, but thin liquidity meant they only netted around $2.8 million after causing a massive price crash.\n\n## Market Impact and Regulatory Fallout\n\nThe attack had an immediate and severe impact on the market value of StablR’s tokens and has triggered what will likely be a significant regulatory test case under new EU digital asset laws. The incident showcases the real-world consequences of both security failures and the enforcement powers of new regulatory frameworks.\n\n### USDR and EURR De-Peg From Fiat\n\nFollowing the exploit and subsequent sell-off, both USDR and EURR lost their pegs. The USDR token, with a pre-attack market cap of $20 million, saw its value drop significantly. While it has partially recovered, it remains below its target dollar parity. The euro-pegged EURR, with a $10 million market cap, fared much worse, collapsing to $0.548 against the euro's value of $1.16.\n\nThis de-pegging has frozen the market, as holders cannot redeem their tokens for the fiat collateral that was supposed to be securing them. The price crash has effectively wiped out millions in holder value and locked up remaining capital in an illiquid market.\n\n### MiCA Compliance Under Scrutiny\n\nStablR’s admission that its tokens are not fully backed is a clear breach of MiCA’s rules. The regulation mandates that issuers hold liquid reserves at a 1:1 ratio with circulating tokens. This rule is the bedrock of stablecoin safety, ensuring holders can redeem their assets at par value at any time.\n\nThe company plans to notify the Malta Financial Services Authority (MFSA) under the reporting rules of MiCA and the EU’s Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA). This case will be one of the first major tests of MiCA’s enforcement powers. Regulators and the market will watch closely to see how StablR addresses the crisis and what precedent is set for handling stablecoin failures in the EU.\n\n## StablR's Response and Industry Context\n\nStablR Chief Executive Officer Gijs op de Weegh stated the company is acting “with full transparency” as the investigation continues. The firm is working with law enforcement and cybersecurity experts to trace the attacker. However, the path to restoring the peg or compensating holders remains uncertain.\n\nThe StablR exploit is a high-profile example in a month that has been rife with crypto hacks. Data platform DefiLlama has tracked over 20 exploits in May 2026 alone. This trend highlights the persistent security challenges in the decentralized finance space and the critical importance of robust, multi-layered security configurations, especially for projects that custody user funds.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe **StablR stablecoin exploit** provides a stark lesson in operational security, showing how a single point of failure in a multisig wallet led to a catastrophic $13.5 million breach. The attack not only de-pegged USDR and EURR but also puts the issuer in direct violation of the EU’s new MiCA regulations. For traders, this incident underscores the necessity of vetting the security architecture behind stablecoins, as not all are built to withstand such threats. The regulatory response from Maltese authorities will now set a crucial precedent for how stablecoin failures are managed under the MiCA framework.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. What was the StablR stablecoin exploit?**\n\nThe StablR stablecoin exploit was a cyberattack where an attacker used a vulnerability to mint $13.5 million in unbacked USDR and EURR tokens. This action caused the stablecoins to become under-collateralized and lose their 1:1 peg to their respective fiat currencies.\n\n**2. How did the attacker compromise StablR's security?**\n\nThe attacker exploited a weak 1-of-3 multisignature wallet. By compromising a single private key, they gained control, locked out the original team, and were able to mint unlimited new tokens.\n\n**3. What is MiCA and how does it relate to this incident?**\n\nMiCA (Markets in Crypto-Assets) is an EU regulation requiring stablecoin issuers like StablR to maintain 1:1 reserves for their tokens. StablR has acknowledged it is in breach of this key rule due to the attack, which will trigger a regulatory response.\n\n**4. Are the USDR and EURR tokens still backed 1:1?**\n\nNo. StablR has confirmed that its USDR and EURR tokens are not currently backed at a 1:1 ratio. The company has suspended redemptions while it investigates the breach and its potential remedies.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779800901779}}
