0M3AmIGY4mbK7-cQH5T98KPwICHm905N8XQkmwWh1rMLzexXGJmCXfwh","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL RULES\n\nFollow these rules for every article. Interior structure (how many sections, headings, hook style) is your editorial decision within the borders below.\n\n---\n\n## Hard rules (never break)\n\n**Content quality**\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use `—`. Use a comma, period, or restructure.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype or fluff (\"amazing\", \"shocking\").\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only, inside H3 sections if you use them. Never in Conclusion or opening hook.\n\n**SEO and links**\n- **ANCHOR LINKS:** Exactly **2** markdown links to URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON.source_urls` (distinct URLs, no repeats). Both in the **opening hook or first H2 only**. No x.com or twitter.com links in the body.\n- **KEYWORDS:** Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words); in the **first sentence of the hook** (the very first line of body text under H1); in at least one H2 and in Conclusion. Use secondary keywords in H2/H3 where natural.\n\n**Length**\n- **Word count:** Body **1000–1200** words (aim **1100**). Count body only; META, Sources, and `[Word Count:]` line are not counted. Body includes hook, H2 sections, Conclusion, and FAQs.\n- **Verify before footer:** Run `python3 ~/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator/skills/pipeline/count_article_body_words.py --path <raw.md>`. The `[Word Count: N]` line must equal the script's `BODY_WORDS` exactly — never guess.\n\n**Fixed section order**\n```\nMETA → H1 (hook) → H2 body sections → ## Conclusion → ## FAQs → **Sources:** → [Word Count: NNNN]\n```\n- **## Conclusion** must appear immediately before **## FAQs**.\n- **## FAQs** must be the **last** section in the content body (immediately before **Sources:**).\n- Do not put FAQs before Conclusion or interleave FAQs in the body.\n\n---\n\n## Structure borders (you decide inside these limits)\n\n| Element | Minimum | Maximum |\n|---------|---------|---------|\n| H2 body sections (excluding Conclusion, FAQs) | 2 | 4 |\n| H3 subsections (`###`) total | 3 | 6 |\n| FAQ items under `## FAQs` | 3 | 6 |\n\nWithin these borders, you choose:\n- How many H2s, H3s, and FAQs to use\n- Heading text and angle for each section\n- How to open the article (2–4 paragraphs under H1)\n- Section depth, bullet lists, and flow\n\nThere is **no** prescribed mapping from fact count to section count. Decide what structure best serves **this** story.\n\nEach H3 should draw on distinct material from research; do not pad with empty sections.\n\n---\n\n## META block (required first)\n\nThe first word of the file must be **META**.\n\nCount characters in `<thinking>` before writing:\n\n| Field | Your max | Rank Math cap |\n|-------|----------|---------------|\n| SEO Title | **55** | 60 |\n| Meta Description | **155** | 160 |\n| URL Slug | **50** | 75 |\n\n```\nMETA\n- SEO Title: [Primary Keyword first (within first 3 words); include one number/figure when factual; ≤55 chars]\n- Meta Description: [Must contain the Primary Keyword verbatim; hook + detail; ≤155 chars]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase hyphens; 3–5 words; ≤50 chars]\n- Categories: [e.g. Regulation, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [...]\n- Secondary Keywords: [...]\n```\n\n---\n\n## H1 and body\n\nAfter META and `---`, write **one** H1:\n\n`# [Primary keyword in first 5 words; include a price or number when relevant]`\n\nThen your hook and H2/H3 body sections. Heading names and section count are your choice within the borders above.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion (required)\n\n`## Conclusion`\n\nRestate the event with primary keyword and actionable close for traders. Length is your call within the total word band.\n\n---\n\n## FAQs (required, last before Sources)\n\n`## FAQs`\n\nWrite questions that fit **this** story (no fixed question bank). **3–6** items within the FAQ border.\n\nFormat each item:\n```\n**1. Your question here?**\n\nAnswer paragraph, 3–4 sentences.\n```\n\nRenumber 1..N. Do not use markdown list syntax for questions.\n\n---\n\n## Sources footer (required)\n\nDo not use `## Sources` or `## Word Count` as headings.\n\n```\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication]: [URL]\n- [Publication]: [URL]\n\n[Word Count: 1100]\n```\n\nFinal line: `[Word Count: NNNN]` with digits only.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-flight (in `<thinking>`)\n\n- [ ] META char counts within limits\n- [ ] Meta Description contains Primary Keyword verbatim\n- [ ] SEO Title starts with Primary Keyword (within first 3 words) and has a number/figure\n- [ ] URL Slug ≤50 chars\n- [ ] First body sentence (under H1) contains Primary Keyword\n- [ ] 2 source URLs planned for hook/first H2\n- [ ] Structure choices within H2/H3/FAQ borders\n- [ ] Body word budget 1000–1200\n- [ ] Order: Conclusion then FAQs then Sources\n\n## Post-output check\n\n- [ ] 1 H1; H2/H3/FAQ counts within borders\n- [ ] Conclusion before FAQs; FAQs before Sources\n- [ ] 1000–1200 body words\n- [ ] Exactly 2 source links in hook/first H2\n- [ ] No em-dashes or banned phrases\n- [ ] Meta Description contains Primary Keyword\n- [ ] First sentence under H1 contains Primary Keyword\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779800937534}}
{"type":"message","id":"269be89b","parentId":"16c3e1ec","timestamp":"2026-05-26T13:09:35.020Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<thinking>\nThe user wants me to revise an article.\nThe revision is triggered by a validation error: `ARTICLE_INVALID: expected 3-6 H3 subsections (###), found 2.`\n\nMy task is to:\n1.  Read the baseline article at `/tmp/crypto-run-20260526-130422/article/final.md`.\n2.  Read the research file at `/tmp/crypto-run-20260526-130422/research/validated.json`.\n3.  Read the template file `COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md`.\n4.  Identify the section with H3s and add one more to meet the `3-6` requirement. The original has two. I need to add at least one more.\n5.  The feedback says \"Preserve body length within ±50 words of the baseline\". I need to be mindful of this.\n6.  The feedback also says \"Do NOT full-rewrite\". I will make a targeted edit.\n7.  I must follow all the rules from `SOUL.md` and `COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md`.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  I have already read the `final.md`, `validated.json`, and `COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md` files.\n2.  The original article has two H3 sections under the \"Market Impact and Regulatory Fallout\" H2:\n    *   `### USDR and EURR De-Peg From Fiat`\n    *   `### MiCA Compliance Under Scrutiny`\n3.  The research mentions that StablR \"asked exchanges to halt trading, deposits and withdrawals for both stablecoins\". This is a distinct point that can be its own H3 subsection. It fits logically within the \"Market Impact\" section.\n4.  I will add a new H3 section: `### Trading Halts and Exchange Reactions`.\n5.  To keep the word count similar, I will take the sentence \"Exchanges have been requested to halt trading while StablR, with the help of [external cybersecurity firms](https://beincrypto.com/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit/), investigates the breach.\" from the intro and move it into this new H3 section, possibly expanding on it slightly based on the research. The research says \"StablR froze token operations and asked exchanges to halt trading, deposits and withdrawals for both stablecoins while the company investigates the breach.\"\n6.  This change is minimal and targeted, as requested. It directly addresses the H3 count issue.\n7.  I will construct the full revised article with this change.\n8.  I will then run the `count_article_body_words.py` script on the new `raw.md`.\n9.  Finally, I'll update the `[Word Count: N]` footer and write the final article to `/tmp/crypto-run-20260526-130422/article/raw.md`.\n\n**Original H2 section:**\n```markdown\n## Market Impact and Regulatory Fallout\n\nThe attack had an immediate and severe impact on the market value of StablR’s tokens and has triggered what will likely be a significant regulatory test case under new EU digital asset laws. The incident showcases the real-world consequences of both security failures and the enforcement powers of new regulatory frameworks.\n\n### USDR and EURR De-Peg From Fiat\n\nFollowing the exploit and subsequent sell-off, both USDR and EURR lost their pegs. The USDR token, with a pre-attack market cap of $20 million, saw its value drop significantly. While it has partially recovered, it remains below its target dollar parity. The euro-pegged EURR, with a $10 million market cap, fared much worse, collapsing to $0.548 against the euro's value of $1.16.\n\nThis de-pegging has frozen the market, as holders cannot redeem their tokens for the fiat collateral that was supposed to be securing them. The price crash has effectively wiped out millions in holder value and locked up remaining capital in an illiquid market.\n\n### MiCA Compliance Under Scrutiny\n\nStablR’s admission that its tokens are not fully backed is a clear breach of MiCA’s rules. The regulation mandates that issuers hold liquid reserves at a 1:1 ratio with circulating tokens. This rule is the bedrock of stablecoin safety, ensuring holders can redeem their assets at par value at any time.\n\nThe company plans to notify the Malta Financial Services Authority (MFSA) under the reporting rules of MiCA and the EU’s Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA). This case will be one of the first major tests of MiCA’s enforcement powers. Regulators and the market will watch closely to see how StablR addresses the crisis and what precedent is set for handling stablecoin failures in the EU.\n```\n\n**Revised H2 section (draft):**\n```markdown\n## Market Impact and Regulatory Fallout\n\nThe attack had an immediate and severe impact on the market value of StablR’s tokens and has triggered what will likely be a significant regulatory test case under new EU digital asset laws. The incident showcases the real-world consequences of both security failures and the enforcement powers of new regulatory frameworks.\n\n### USDR and EURR De-Peg From Fiat\n\nFollowing the exploit and subsequent sell-off, both USDR and EURR lost their pegs. The USDR token, with a pre-attack market cap of $20 million, saw its value drop significantly. While it has partially recovered, it remains below its target dollar parity. The euro-pegged EURR, with a $10 million market cap, fared much worse, collapsing to $0.548 against the euro's value of $1.16.\n\nThis de-pegging has frozen the market, as holders cannot redeem their tokens for the fiat collateral that was supposed to be securing them. The price crash has effectively wiped out millions in holder value and locked up remaining capital in an illiquid market.\n\n### Trading Halts and Exchange Reactions\n\nIn response to the crisis, StablR took immediate action to contain the fallout. The company confirmed it had requested that all cryptocurrency exchanges halt trading, deposits, and withdrawals for both USDR and EURR. This move aims to prevent the attacker from cashing out further illicitly minted tokens and to stabilize the market by freezing activity. The freeze locks in existing holders, however, leaving them unable to exit their positions until the situation is resolved.\n\n### MiCA Compliance Under Scrutiny\n\nStablR’s admission that its tokens are not fully backed is a clear breach of MiCA’s rules. The regulation mandates that issuers hold liquid reserves at a 1:1 ratio with circulating tokens. This rule is the bedrock of stablecoin safety, ensuring holders can redeem their assets at par value at any time.\n\nThe company plans to notify the Malta Financial Services Authority (MFSA) under the reporting rules of MiCA and the EU’s Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA). This case will be one of the first major tests of MiCA’s enforcement powers. Regulators and the market will watch closely to see how StablR addresses the crisis and what precedent is set for handling stablecoin failures in the EU.\n```\nThis looks good. It adds a new H3, uses information from the research, and should keep the word count change minimal. I will now construct the entire article. I'll need to remove the sentence about exchanges from the intro.\n\nOriginal intro sentence to remove: \"Exchanges have been requested to halt trading while StablR, with the help of [external cybersecurity firms](https://beincrypto.com/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit/), investigates the breach.\"\nThe link from that sentence is one of the two required source links. I must ensure it remains in the article. The other link is to CoinDesk. I will move the beincrypto link into the first H2 section, as per the rules.\n\nLet's check the rules again: \"exactly 2 markdown links to URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON.source_urls` (distinct URLs, no repeats). Both in the opening hook or first H2 only.\"\n\nOriginal Intro:\n> The **StablR stablecoin exploit** has forced the European issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its USDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According to a [statement from the company](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/26/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens), the move came after an attacker leveraged a critical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked tokens.\n>\n> The breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident places StablR’s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the European Union’s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into full effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens are no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA’s core requirements. Exchanges have been requested to halt trading while StablR, with the help of [external cybersecurity firms](https://beincrypto.com/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit/), investigates the breach.\n\nRevised Intro:\n> The **StablR stablecoin exploit** has forced the European issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its USDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According to a [statement from the company](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/26/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens), the move came after an attacker leveraged a critical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked tokens.\n>\n> The breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident places StablR’s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the European Union’s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into full effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens are no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA’s core requirements, and is working with [external cybersecurity firms](https://beincrypto.com/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit/) to investigate the breach.\n\nThis keeps both links in the intro/first H2 area as required, and removes the specific sentence about exchanges which is now in its own H3. This seems like a solid plan.\n\nNow, I'll write the full revised article content.\nI will then use the `write` tool to save it.\nThen I will use `exec` to run the word count script.\nThen I will use `edit` to update the word count in the file.\nFinally, I will yield \"SUCCESS\".\n\nFull revised content construction:\n\n**META:** (No changes)\n- SEO Title: StablR Stablecoin Exploit: Attacker Mints $13.5M\n- Meta Description: The StablR stablecoin exploit has frozen USDR and EURR trading after an attacker minted $13.5M in unbacked tokens, violating MiCA's 1:1 backing rule.\n- URL Slug: stablr-stablecoin-exploit-freezes-tokens\n- Categories: Stablecoins, Security, Regulation\n- Primary Keyword: StablR stablecoin exploit\n- Secondary Keywords: MiCA regulation, stablecoin depeg, multisig wallet security\n---\n**H1:**\n# StablR Stablecoin Exploit Freezes USDR, EURR After $13.5M Breach\n---\n**Body (Intro):**\nThe **StablR stablecoin exploit** has forced the European issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its USDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According to a [statement from the company](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/26/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens), the move came after an attacker leveraged a critical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked tokens.\n\nThe breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident places StablR’s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the European Union’s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into full effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens are no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA’s core requirements, and is working with [external cybersecurity firms](https://beincrypto.com/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit/) to investigate the breach.\n---\n**Body (H2s/H3s):**\n## How a Weak Multisig Led to a $13.5M Exploit\n(No changes to this section)\n\n## Market Impact and Regulatory Fallout\nThe attack had an immediate and severe impact on the market value of StablR’s tokens and has triggered what will likely be a significant regulatory test case under new EU digital asset laws. The incident showcases the real-world consequences of both security failures and the enforcement powers of new regulatory frameworks.\n\n### USDR and EURR De-Peg From Fiat\nFollowing the exploit and subsequent sell-off, both USDR and EURR lost their pegs. The USDR token, with a pre-attack market cap of $20 million, saw its value drop significantly. While it has partially recovered, it remains below its target dollar parity. The euro-pegged EURR, with a $10 million market cap, fared much worse, collapsing to $0.548 against the euro's value of $1.16.\n\nThis de-pegging has frozen the market, as holders cannot redeem their tokens for the fiat collateral that was supposed to be securing them. The price crash has effectively wiped out millions in holder value and locked up remaining capital in an illiquid market.\n\n### Trading Halts and Exchange Reactions\nIn response to the crisis, StablR took immediate action to contain the fallout. The company confirmed it had requested that all cryptocurrency exchanges halt trading, deposits, and withdrawals for both USDR and EURR. This move aims to prevent the attacker from cashing out further illicitly minted tokens and to stabilize the market by freezing activity. The freeze locks in existing holders, however, leaving them unable to exit their positions until the situation is resolved.\n\n### MiCA Compliance Under Scrutiny\nStablR’s admission that its tokens are not fully backed is a clear breach of MiCA’s rules. The regulation mandates that issuers hold liquid reserves at a 1:1 ratio with circulating tokens. This rule is the bedrock of stablecoin safety, ensuring holders can redeem their assets at par value at any time.\n\nThe company plans to notify the Malta Financial Services Authority (MFSA) under the reporting rules of MiCA and the EU’s Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA). This case will be one of the first major tests of MiCA’s enforcement powers. Regulators and the market will watch closely to see how StablR addresses the crisis and what precedent is set for handling stablecoin failures in the EU.\n---\n**Body (Rest):**\n(No changes to \"StablR's Response and Industry Context\", \"Conclusion\", or \"FAQs\" sections.)\n\nI will now write this full content to the file."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"write","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"META\n- SEO Title: StablR Stablecoin Exploit: Attacker Mints $13.5M\n- Meta Description: The StablR stablecoin exploit has frozen USDR and EURR trading after an attacker minted $13.5M in unbacked tokens, violating MiCA's 1:1 backing rule.\n- URL Slug: stablr-stablecoin-exploit-freezes-tokens\n- Categories: Stablecoins, Security, Regulation\n- Primary Keyword: StablR stablecoin exploit\n- Secondary Keywords: MiCA regulation, stablecoin depeg, multisig wallet security\n---\n# StablR Stablecoin Exploit Freezes USDR, EURR After $13.5M Breach\n\nThe **StablR stablecoin exploit** has forced the European issuer to suspend all minting and redemption services for its USDR and EURR tokens, leaving the assets under-collateralized. According to a [statement from the company](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/26/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens), the move came after an attacker leveraged a critical security flaw to mint approximately $13.5 million in unbacked tokens.\n\nThe breach was first flagged by onchain analyst ZachXBT. The incident places StablR’s operations under intense scrutiny, particularly as the European Union’s Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) regulation comes into full effect. The company, based in Malta, has acknowledged its tokens are no longer backed 1:1, a direct violation of MiCA’s core requirements, and is working with [external cybersecurity firms](https://beincrypto.com/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit/) to investigate the breach.\n\n## How a Weak Multisig Led to a $13.5M Exploit\n\nThe root cause of the attack was a fundamental weakness in StablR’s smart contract architecture on Ethereum. Blockchain security firm GoPlus Security identified a poorly configured multisignature wallet as the single point of failure. This wallet, responsible for minting new tokens, used a 1-of-3 signature threshold. This meant any transaction could be approved by just one of the three authorized signers, offering minimal protection.\n\nThe attacker compromised one of these keys, granting them unilateral control. They added their own address as an administrator, removed the legitimate signers, and seized the minting mechanism. With this control, the attacker minted 8.35 million USDR and 4.5 million EURR. These illicitly created tokens, worth $13.5 million on paper, immediately diluted the value of the legitimately backed supply. The attacker then attempted to sell the tokens on decentralized exchanges, but thin liquidity meant they only netted around $2.8 million after causing a massive price crash.\n\n## Market Impact and Regulatory Fallout\n\nThe attack had an immediate and severe impact on the market value of StablR’s tokens and has triggered what will likely be a significant regulatory test case under new EU digital asset laws. The incident showcases the real-world consequences of both security failures and the enforcement powers of new regulatory frameworks.\n\n### USDR and EURR De-Peg From Fiat\n\nFollowing the exploit and subsequent sell-off, both USDR and EURR lost their pegs. The USDR token, with a pre-attack market cap of $20 million, saw its value drop significantly. While it has partially recovered, it remains below its target dollar parity. The euro-pegged EURR, with a $10 million market cap, fared much worse, collapsing to $0.548 against the euro's value of $1.16.\n\nThis de-pegging has frozen the market, as holders cannot redeem their tokens for the fiat collateral that was supposed to be securing them. The price crash has effectively wiped out millions in holder value and locked up remaining capital in an illiquid market.\n\n### Trading Halts and Exchange Reactions\n\nIn response to the crisis, StablR took immediate action to contain the fallout. The company confirmed it had requested that all cryptocurrency exchanges halt trading, deposits, and withdrawals for both USDR and EURR. This move aims to prevent the attacker from cashing out further illicitly minted tokens and to stabilize the market by freezing activity. The freeze locks in existing holders, however, leaving them unable to exit their positions until the situation is resolved.\n\n### MiCA Compliance Under Scrutiny\n\nStablR’s admission that its tokens are not fully backed is a clear breach of MiCA’s rules. The regulation mandates that issuers hold liquid reserves at a 1:1 ratio with circulating tokens. This rule is the bedrock of stablecoin safety, ensuring holders can redeem their assets at par value at any time.\n\nThe company plans to notify the Malta Financial Services Authority (MFSA) under the reporting rules of MiCA and the EU’s Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA). This case will be one of the first major tests of MiCA’s enforcement powers. Regulators and the market will watch closely to see how StablR addresses the crisis and what precedent is set for handling stablecoin failures in the EU.\n\n## StablR's Response and Industry Context\n\nStablR Chief Executive Officer Gijs op de Weegh stated the company is acting “with full transparency” as the investigation continues. The firm is working with law enforcement and cybersecurity experts to trace the attacker. However, the path to restoring the peg or compensating holders remains uncertain.\n\nThe StablR exploit is a high-profile example in a month that has been rife with crypto hacks. Data platform DefiLlama has tracked over 20 exploits in May 2026 alone. This trend highlights the persistent security challenges in the decentralized finance space and the critical importance of robust, multi-layered security configurations, especially for projects that custody user funds.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe **StablR stablecoin exploit** provides a stark lesson in operational security, showing how a single point of failure in a multisig wallet led to a catastrophic $13.5 million breach. The attack not only de-pegged USDR and EURR but also puts the issuer in direct violation of the EU’s new MiCA regulations. For traders, this incident underscores the necessity of vetting the security architecture behind stablecoins, as not all are built to withstand such threats. The regulatory response from Maltese authorities will now set a crucial precedent for how stablecoin failures are managed under the MiCA framework.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. What was the StablR stablecoin exploit?**\n\nThe StablR stablecoin exploit was a cyberattack where an attacker used a vulnerability to mint $13.5 million in unbacked USDR and EURR tokens. This action caused the stablecoins to become under-collateralized and lose their 1:1 peg to their respective fiat currencies.\n\n**2. How did the attacker compromise StablR's security?**\n\nThe attacker exploited a weak 1-of-3 multisignature wallet. By compromising a single private key, they gained control, locked out the original team, and were able to mint unlimited new tokens.\n\n**3. What is MiCA and how does it relate to this incident?**\n\nMiCA (Markets in Crypto-Assets) is an EU regulation requiring stablecoin issuers like StablR to maintain 1:1 reserves for their tokens. StablR has acknowledged it is in breach of this key rule due to the attack, which will trigger a regulatory response.\n\n**4. Are the USDR and EURR tokens still backed 1:1?**\n\nNo. StablR has confirmed that its USDR and EURR tokens are not currently backed at a 1:1 ratio. The company has suspended redemptions while it investigates the breach and its potential remedies.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/26/stablr-freezes-usdr-and-eurr-after-attacker-mints-usd13-5-million-in-unbacked-tokens\n- BeInCrypto: https://beincrypto.com/stablr-stablecoin-depeg-exploit/\n\n[Word Count: 1045]\n","path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260526-130422/article/raw.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gemini-2.5-pro","usage":{"input":10124,"output":5223,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":15347,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779800937537,"responseId":"apsVatGmKp3A0r4PmIT2uQk"}}
